add("## Abstract\n")
add(f"**Question.** Do salivary estradiol and progesterone improve the prediction of mood beyond cycle phase, in women the model has never seen? **Data.** Open data of Pletzer & Noachtar (2023): {n_rows('pooled')} sessions from {n_wom('pooled')} women ({n_rows('long')} sessions from {n_wom('long')} longitudinal women with up to three sessions at menses, peri-ovulation and mid-luteal phase, and {n_rows('cross')} sessions from {n_wom('cross')} cross-sectional women with one session each). "
    f"**Methods.** Ridge regression implemented from scratch (normal equations, SVD, gradient methods), compared with random forests and gradient boosting, under leave-one-woman-out cross-validation with nested hyper-parameter selection and women-level bootstrap intervals; mixed-effects models and permutation tests; a negative control and a sanity check. "
    f"**Results.** Phase alone explained little out of sample (R2 versus the mean baseline, DRSP psychological symptoms, ridge: {cA['drsp_psych']}; positive affect {cA['pos_affect']}; state anxiety {cA['stai']}). "
    f"Adding hormones to phase never helped: in {n_ca_all} paired comparisons none showed a significant improvement and {n_ca_worse} were significantly worse. Questionnaire scores (BDI, BAI, PSST) carried most of the predictable signal (DRSP, ridge, set D: {cD['drsp_psych']}). "
    f"In-sample, phase and within-woman estradiol effects were clear (mixed models, longitudinal sample, DRSP psychological: luteal minus menses {mxci('long','drsp_psych','phase','ph2')} SD), consistent with the authors, and the estradiol effect was entirely within-woman. "
    f"Hormones did identify the phase (balanced accuracy {ci(B6, dict(sample='pooled', model='logreg'), 'bal_acc', '{:.2f}', label='phase from hormones, pooled, logistic')} versus chance {calc('chance balanced accuracy', 1/3, '{:.3f}', 'definition', '1/number of classes (3)')}), and failed to predict emotion-recognition accuracy as expected. "
    f"Conclusion: on this sample, hormones add no detectable predictive value for mood in unseen women beyond cycle phase; significant group-level effects do not translate into individual-level prediction.\n")
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Introduction
section("Introduction"); add("## 1. Introduction\n")
add(f"Mood is often said to vary with the menstrual cycle. A statistical effect on average (\"mood is better in the peri-ovulatory phase than at menses\") is different from the ability to predict one woman's mood from her cycle information. This project asks the predictive question on open data of Pletzer & Noachtar (2023, *Hormones and Behavior*), where the authors reported group-level phase and estradiol effects on mood. The course context (Mathematics Behind AI) is the second aim: the core methods are implemented from scratch in NumPy (least squares and ridge via normal equations and SVD, gradient and Hessian, condition number, GD / SGD / momentum / Adam, ridge as a Bayesian MAP estimate) and then used in a leakage-aware evaluation. "
    f"The research question is: **do salivary estradiol and progesterone improve prediction of mood beyond cycle phase alone, on women the model has never seen?**\n")
