# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Discussion etc.
section("Discussion"); add("## 5. Discussion\n")
add(f"The study separates two questions. At the **group level** the data reproduce the authors' findings: mood and symptoms differ by phase and within-woman estradiol tracks lower DRSP symptoms. At the **individual level** (an unseen woman) phase and hormones give at most a small gain for premenstrual symptoms and none for affect or anxiety, and hormones add nothing to phase. The reasons are structural: (i) between-woman differences dominate (ICC {val(ICC, dict(sample='long', target='drsp_psych'), 'ICC_anova', '{:.2f}', label='ICC long drsp')}-{val(ICC, dict(sample='long', target='stai'), 'ICC_anova', '{:.2f}', label='ICC long stai')} in the longitudinal sample) and cannot be predicted from cycle information; (ii) the estradiol effect is a within-woman deviation from the woman's own level, which a model of a new woman cannot use; (iii) estradiol and phase are strongly entangled, so there is little information in estradiol beyond phase; (iv) the group-level phase effects are small relative to the noise (marginal R2 of phase {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='drsp_psych'), 'marginal_R2_phase', '{:.3f}', label='marginal R2 DRSP long')} for DRSP psychological, {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='stai'), 'marginal_R2_phase', '{:.3f}', label='marginal R2 stai long')} for anxiety). "
    f"Knowing a woman's own earlier reports helps much more than any hormone (leave-one-session-out: {lo_('drsp_psych', 'ridge C + personal mean')} versus {lo_('drsp_psych', 'ridge C (no history)')}), which suggests that personalised, longitudinal tracking, not hormone assays, is the promising direction. The credibility checks support the pipeline: it finds the phase from hormones (balanced accuracy {bl('pooled', 'logreg', 'bal_acc', '{:.2f}')} versus chance 0.33) and finds no skill for emotion-recognition accuracy; the speed result is the one nominal exception.\n")
section("Limitations"); add("## 6. Limitations\n")
add(f"- **Small samples.** Only {n_wom('long')} women contribute repeated measures and {n_wom('cross')} contribute one session; bootstrap intervals are wide, and small true effects cannot be excluded. The pooled sample mixes the two kinds of women.\n- **Noisy hormones.** Salivary immunoassays have limited validity (the authors say so too); measurement error attenuates any true hormone effect, and {dcalc('exact zero E2 readings', int((A.estradiol==0).sum()), how='count estradiol==0')} readings of estradiol are exactly zero.\n- **Concurrent, not forecasting.** Hormones and mood are measured in the same session; the models describe a state, they do not predict the future. Phase labels come from the study schedule.\n- **Self-report overlap.** BDI, BAI, PSST, DRSP, PANAS and STAI are all self-reports and conceptually overlap, so the strong skill of set D is 'one questionnaire explains another', not early warning.\n- **Differences from the paper.** My DRSP target (psychological sum) and models differ from the authors' (their DRSP mean score per symptom type, nlme models); p-values use Wald z instead of nlme t-tests; the PSST interaction is weaker for my score; the emotion-recognition outcomes were simplified.\n- **No external validation.** Everything is cross-validated within one study; a different population or assay could behave differently.\n- **Multiple testing.** Several targets, models, samples and sets were examined; corrections were applied to the paired hormone comparisons only.\n- **Learning project, not a clinical tool.** Nothing here should be used to diagnose, treat or advise.\n")
section("Conclusion"); add("## 7. Conclusion\n")
add(f"On {nn('pooled')} from the open data of Pletzer & Noachtar (2023), salivary estradiol and progesterone did **not** improve the prediction of positive affect, DRSP psychological symptoms or state anxiety in unseen women beyond cycle phase (0 of {n_ca_all} paired comparisons improved; {n_ca_worse} got worse), even though phase and within-woman estradiol effects are clearly present in-sample. Person-level questionnaire scores and, above all, a woman's own history are far more informative. The from-scratch ridge machinery matches scikit-learn, its optimisation behaviour is explained by the Hessian's condition number, and the pipeline passes a negative control (with one small speed caveat) and a positive sanity check.\n")
add("<!-- EXTENSION SLOT: sections for Phase 7 extensions are written by src/ext_*_report.py (numbers loaded from results/ext*.csv) and by any docs/extensions/*.md -->\n")
EXT = []
for _f in sorted((ROOT / "src").glob("ext_*_report.py")): exec(open(_f).read())
ext = sorted((ROOT / "docs/extensions").glob("*.md")) if (ROOT / "docs/extensions").exists() else []
if EXT or ext:
    add("## 8. Extensions\n"); add("*Phase 7 extensions, one note per method in `docs/math/`; same leave-one-participant-out protocol, same seed, same women; all numbers loaded from `results/ext*.csv`.*\n")
    [add(e) for e in EXT]; [add(p.read_text() + "\n") for p in ext]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Summary of findings (inserted at the top; every number loaded)
section("Summary of findings")
_minp = calc('min raw one-sided p C vs A', df(MC)[df(MC).family == 'C vs A'].p_improve_one_sided.min(), '{:.3f}', MC, 'min p_improve_one_sided in family C vs A')
SUMMARY = f"""## Summary of findings

**The question.** Mood is often said to follow the menstrual cycle. This project asks a practical version: if we know a woman's cycle phase and the estradiol and progesterone in her saliva, can we predict her mood better than by simply guessing the average, for a woman the model has never seen? Data: open study data of Pletzer & Noachtar (2023), {nn('pooled')} (of which {nn('long')} are women with up to three sessions).

**Five key results.**
1. *Hormones add nothing to cycle phase for unseen women.* Of {n_ca_all} paired comparisons (phase + hormones versus phase alone) {n_ca_better} showed an improvement and {n_ca_worse} were worse; the smallest uncorrected p-value for an improvement was {_minp}.
2. *Phase alone helps only a little, and only for premenstrual symptoms.* Share of the error removed compared with guessing the average (ridge, pooled): DRSP psychological symptoms {cA['drsp_psych']}, positive affect {cA['pos_affect']}, state anxiety {cA['stai']}.
3. *Questionnaires, and above all the woman's own history, carry the predictable signal.* With BDI, BAI and PSST added the DRSP figure rises to {cD['drsp_psych']}; giving the model a woman's own mean from her other sessions lifted a phase-plus-hormone model from {lo_('drsp_psych', 'ridge C (no history)')} to {lo_('drsp_psych', 'ridge C + personal mean')} (longitudinal women).
4. *Group-level effects are real but small, and they happen within a woman.* In-sample, DRSP symptoms are lower in the luteal phase than at menses ({mxci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'phase', 'ph2')} standard deviations, as in the authors' paper), and the estradiol link is within a woman ({mxci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'E2 within/between', 'zw_estradiol')}) but absent between women ({mxci('long', 'drsp_psych', 'E2 within/between', 'zb_estradiol')}). Phase explains only {val(R2P, dict(sample='long', outcome='drsp_psych'), 'marginal_R2_phase', '{:.3f}', label='marginal R2 DRSP long')} of the variance, which is why significant averages do not give individual predictions.
5. *The checks behave as they should.* Hormones identify the cycle phase (balanced accuracy {ci(B6, dict(sample='pooled', model='logreg'), 'bal_acc', '{:.2f}', lo='bal_acc_lo', hi='bal_acc_hi', label='balacc pooled logreg')} versus chance {calc('chance', 1/3, '{:.3f}', 'definition', '1/3 for 3 classes')}); emotion-recognition accuracy, which should be unrelated, is not predicted (best R2 {calc('negctrl accuracy max R2_oos', hs.R2_oos.max(), '{:+.3f}', N6, 'max R2_oos, target er_accuracy, sets B/C/D')}), with one small, uncorrected caveat for reaction speed.

The extensions (section 8) agree: the Bayesian ridge predicts like ridge (largest RMSE difference {calc('E2 max abs dRMSE', float(rv.delta.abs().max()), '{:.3f}', E2V, 'max |delta|, metric RMSE')}); 90% prediction intervals for new women of the same population cover {mcl('pooled', 'bayes_predictive', 0.9)} (Bayesian) and {mcl('pooled', 'jackknife_conformal', 0.9)} (conformal) of sessions but are wide and fail across samples; dropping the hormones from the full model costs nothing; and random forests and boosting fit their training women far better than new women (train-minus-test R2 up to {calc('OF largest gap', float(df(OF).gap.max()), '{:.3f}', OF, 'max gap')}) without testing better than ridge. Cross-sample validation (E7: training on the single-session women and testing on the longitudinal women, and the reverse) gives the same picture: the questionnaire model transfers in both directions (DRSP, ridge, set D: R2 {ci(E7['metrics'], dict(sample='cross_to_long', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='D'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E7 ridge D drsp dir1')} and {ci(E7['metrics'], dict(sample='long_to_cross', target='drsp_psych', model='ridge', fset='D'), 'R2_oos', '{:+.3f}', label='E7 ridge D drsp dir2')}), while hormones improved prediction in neither direction after Holm and FDR correction (smallest adjusted p for an improvement {calc('E7 min Holm improve all', float(HT.p_holm_all_improve.min()), '{:.3f}', E7['hormone_tests'], 'min p_holm_all_improve')}); this is validation across two groups of women of the same study, not across datasets.

**Three main limitations.** (1) *Small samples:* only {n_wom('long')} women contribute repeated sessions and {n_wom('cross')} contribute one, so intervals are wide and small effects cannot be excluded. (2) *Noisy hormone measurements:* salivary immunoassays have limited validity, which could hide a real effect. (3) *Concurrent, not forecasting:* hormones and mood are measured in the same session, so the models describe a state rather than predict tomorrow. This is a learning project, not a medical tool.
"""
L.insert(2, SUMMARY)
(ROOT / "docs/report.md").write_text("\n".join(L))
exec(open(ROOT / "src/viva_content.py").read())
exec(open(ROOT / "src/readme_content.py").read())
